A||N||20||grinthock||What's your definition of RED LINE||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||06-25-2001||08:20 AM||||This is a strange question, I would like people's advice/thoughts on this one.  How do you actually define RED LINE?  How does one decide where this "LINE" resides?  What happens to this redline when parts are changed?  What INCREASES redline?<P>Thoughts?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://grinthock.2y.net/pics/87gt/S_P000386.JPG">  <P>Home page: <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com<BR>Home" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroworld.com<BR>Home</A>  of the fiero voting booth!<BR>Owner of 85 GT<BR>87 GT Silver<BR>1995 Ford Escort Wagoon POS||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||06-25-2001||08:52 AM||||Redline is the maximum safe operating speed of the engine under load (i.e. in gear).  The factory determines the redline on each engine through their own testing, and this is the best baseline to start with.  You can sometimes go slightly past this, but you are risking engine failure.  Also, adding hi-perf parts such as cam, lifter, <B>valve springs</B> can increase the useable rpm range, but may put too much stress on the crank and connecting rods, etc.  The only real way for an owner to determine the redline, is to blow the engine up and then set the redline about 100rpm lower.  Since that's not very useful, trial and error, and other people's experiance are a good rule of thumb.  ||||reg||6
Z||000002||84Bill||06-25-2001||08:59 AM||||I noticed if I run my duke at "Redline" the gas mileage plummets to the basement. So IMO Redline not only is the mechanical limit but the limit at witch the engine will perform at an optimum level .||||reg||1
