Z||000010||88formula||06-25-2001||04:46 PM|||| The engine redline is based on a few things.<P> The engine rpm limit is where the RPM limit of the valve train and camshaft are. There’s no use in making an engine rev to 6000 rpm if it only makes power up to 5000 rpm. If the camshaft is designed to make power from idle to 5000 rpm or if the valve springs are light duty than revving the engine beyond that point can cause valve float or the engine won’t make any power. The piston could hit a valve because the valve springs are not strong enough to keep the valves from bouncing against their seats at high rpm’s.<P>The design of the intake and exhaust system has an effect on engine rpm limit. Tuned Port injection with its long runners doesn't support high RPM airflow. It’s designed for low rpm airflow.<P>The strength of the bottom end limits how much power the engine can handle and how high it can rev.<P>The cylinder head design and size of the intake and exhaust runners limits airflow at high rpm’s or low rpm’s. It’s a compromise<P>There is no one answer to this question because everything in an engine is designed to work as a system. Some engines are designed to make more low end power; some mid ranged power, and some high end power. Most engines are compromises in one way or another. If you increase the rpm power and limit of an engine you usually decrease its low rpm power.   <P>To increase rpm range you have to first have an induction system and exhaust system to support it. The cylinder heads have to have larger runners. The camshaft has to have more duration and lift. The valve springs need to be stiffer the lighter. The valves have to be bigger. The intake manifold has to have sorter runners. Usually headers are needed. The connecting rods have to be strong and the rod bolts have to be really strong. Lighter and stronger pistons are needed. The reciprocating assembly needs to be light and strong. Everything has to be perfectly balanced. The list goes on. <P>Most GM production engines are made with cheap parts and are not designed for high performance usage. GM puts cheap heavy pistons in there engines, cheap valve train parts, cheap bolts, cheap connecting rods, cast iron crankshafts, the machine work is half ass, and the balance of the engine is usually off. <P>They are getting better though but there still not up to the quality of the imports. <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Butter||06-25-2001||05:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>This is a strange question, I would like people's advice/thoughts on this one.  How do you actually define RED LINE?  How does one decide where this "LINE" resides?  What happens to this redline when parts are changed?  What INCREASES redline?<P>Thoughts?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I am always open to new and different Ideas.<BR>This is my opinion, ideas and thoughts on the subject.<P>Red line is the max rpm's that can be run continously with no damage.<BR>Manufactors normally give a good starting point for this.<P>When I build an engine and go to raising the red line I like to start by putting in some better rated pistons like hyperutectic or forged.  This is just a starting point for the bottom end.  Connecting rods and the crank must be improved if you plan on raising the R's a bunch over stock.<P>The cam profile and vavle train is probally the biggest factor in determining what the engine's basic red line will be.  <P>The head style, valve size, chambering and porting have more to do with how the cam's ratings are turned on. <P>All this and some more must be designed to work together to operate smoothly. Certain engine block designs have things that must also be adressed like oiling and cooling particulars.  I'm sure there is as many approaches to raising R's as they are people on this forum.  The above ideas and a lot of reseach for parts that work well together go into the engines I build.  <P>Hope you can get some useable info from this.  It's fun to feel a modified engine come to life. <BR>    ||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrgone||06-25-2001||06:05 PM||||OOOPSIE<P>ive had my duke up near 6000<P>well now....||||reg||1
Z||000013||84Bill||06-25-2001||06:59 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrgone:<BR><B>OOOPSIE<P>ive had my duke up near 6000<P>well now....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>OH NO!!! and it didn't blow up! hehehe<BR>The redline is the point at witch the engine is no longer working efficiently enough. Valves will start to float, oil pump will cavitate, volumetric efficiency will fall off rapidly and in some cases catastrophic failures can result from the above mentioned. Lets face it, the engines we run are all capable of above the redline operation. There is and will always be a built in safty cusion. My engine is pushing 120,000 miles. Even pushing it as hard as I do it still purrs at an idle.<BR>But the future can hold many things.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I know some of you have seen this before but I don't go this high very often. I ran at 4800 RPM (yellow arc)  80 MPH almost all the way to Carlisle 120 odd miles almost 2.5 hours.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/Shows/carlisle/image002.jpg"> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-26-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000014||Old Lar||06-25-2001||09:03 PM||||I have driven my 87 5 speed up to where the rev limiter hits, but I have done this in second gear (60 mph)..I figure that I must have been over the red line point.  The 88 auto shifts at 5200 rpm (3.4L) in first when floored.  I don't know how fast that is because it happens too fast.<P>My 3800 s/c Buick is rated at 240 HP at 5200 rpm.  I rarely drive that at 5200 rpm.  Red line 6000 rpm.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Slammed Fiero||06-25-2001||10:12 PM||||Redline in a fiero is around 6300rpm<BR>you can safely run 6500rpm before valve flot. Several ways to get around the factory limiter. While you do have a cold air setup and a cam all which are good for a higher rev limit , your valvetrain is stock and will "float" at higher RPM. Balancing hte motor is a major factor in raising the RPM limit. But balancing alone won't make much of a difference. While you would be able to rev the engine higher without incident of wierd vibrations , your heads (complete) would not create ample enough air flow at extreme RPM's to make a balanced engine useful.<P>Everything builds. A mild street cam doesn't create that much power , about 6-8hp. Throw some hi po heads on that , and you will have higher useable power.<P><BR>To build a serious hi RPM motor , you need to start from scratch. Fieor's willrev to 6500rpm , but I wouldn't want to do it for more than a few seconds. It's not creating any power up there   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals. &lt;b&gt; New White Face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS&lt;/b&gt;Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000016||artherd||06-26-2001||04:59 AM||||Well, I can say that I've had my 87 duke up to 4500-5000 many times and it's been quite reliable.<P>Then the piece of ****  blew a connecting rod RIGHT THROUGH THE BLOCK.<P>Worst sound you'll ever hear.  And after hearing it two more times, I've had it with fiero engines.<P>They're great cars, WONDERFUL cars infact!!<P>But man, it's a shame about that 'engine'.<P>If only the Quad4 or 3.4TDC (now there we go :) would have made it into these cars, it'd be a different world...<P>Best!<BR>Ben.||||reg||3
Z||000017||87GTZ34||06-26-2001||08:55 AM||||Raising RPM in a motor is a concerted and complete undertaking.  1st is reliability and then ability.  Reliability includes things bast the bottom end to strengthed valve retainers & keepers.  It also includes all rotating mass parts for instance.  You balance the engine but what about clutch, tranny parts, the harmonic balancer.  The factory balancer probably shouldn't be revved much past 6500.  On my 3.4, I have a cast reluctor one of the crank signals.  I don't think I trust that one revving to 7 or 7.5K.  Once all of the mechanical pieces are taken care of then it is a matter of ability.  The 2.8 top end was definitely not designed for high RPM operation.  Just trace out all of the air bends from the intake to the intake port.  Once you get it to have power way up there, I don't think it would be very fun to drive in the street either unless you put in more gears (maybe even more than 6) that are close ratio.  What redline did you want to achieve?||||reg||1
Z||000018||grinthock||06-26-2001||09:29 AM||||MY goal in this discussion was not to raise my redline, infact I have no interest in running my car near red, not too great an idea for the engine.  The question was more of a "What determines redline" maybe that would have been a better subject.  I never take my car beyond 5K.  Infact It never goes much beyond 4.5K<P>I agree Slammed about my exisiting mods, they make little to no difference to redline, I would think the heads would be restrictive at high RPM, I mean honestly how many CFM do you need at 7,000 RPM !  What options do we have for upgrading our intake system?  (beyond the basic K&N deal, let's get serious here!)  I have considered a port/polish but then you are getting into some major work.  Balancing is a good idea, but again expensive.   I would agree, if your goal is 7,000 RPM (Is it EVEN acheivable on this stupid 2.8 or why not just do a engine transplant!) then get another engine!<P>What options do we have on the intake side?||||reg||1
Z||000019||fierohoho||06-26-2001||10:24 AM||||Redline is what a ricer leaves after he blows his tranny trying to keep up with a Fiero.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000020||Slammed Fiero||06-26-2001||10:51 AM||||Ford was the first to use a process called extrude honing (1st ona  mass production basis)<P>This is where a Sticky , abrasive paste is sent through the manifold under high pressure. It polishes and removes casting defects , reported to increase airflow on average by about 20%. Really the bottle neck manifold in a fiero is a restictor in getting air into the motor. Cwest i believe is working on a twin TB setup which for V6 monkiers would seem like the way to go.<P>I have seen people adapt TPI TB's to the fiero's manifold , I have seen the upper manifold turned into a large box (ala Accel superram / lingenfelter manifolds) all in attempts to increase air flow.<P>I have 2 spare manifolds at home and had thought of cutting both "Necks" off each and having them grafted onto the manifold so that I could run Twin TB's. Cookstown Autowreckers in Barrie sold me both manifolds and an extra TB for $80 Canadian. The cost isn't in gethering the parts , it's in the labour. Engineering such a project would be time consuming as well as exhausting financialy. I would wait until chris west gets his done.<P>The extrude hone process runs around $300US for the manifolds. And while it does create power. I thinkt he money would be better spent on valve train, and building a set of Hi po heads.<P><BR>The cost of good used 2.8 heads is miniscule (Under $100 canadian for a set) and there is a lot of power hiding in them. SS valves , stiffer crane springs , a good port job . I have heard a good set of heads can yield 25Hp . Which is a real difference since most of us struggle to get 10hp over stock.<P>If I were sticking with a 2.8 , I would get a set of heads and go from there. <P>Hey someone in TO has a 3.1ltr for sale for $300. You can't beat extra Cubes.<P>But for the money , a workbench , a dremel drill with various bits and some time on a set of heads is probably the way to go.  I did this with a 2.3ltr head on my old merkur, took around 10 hrs to do the head , but what a difference. Patience is all it takes. Don't go hog wild with a dremel and take out something that is suppose to be there.<P>Just takes time, lotsa sites on the net give info about how to build a hi power set of heads.<P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals. &lt;b&gt; New White Face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS&lt;/b&gt;Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
